<p>I=E2=80=99m particularly happy with the <em>edge-triggered</em> state tr=
ansition
mechanism. The sign of the state tracks whether the =E2=80=9Csignal=E2=80=
=9D is =E2=80=9Chigh=E2=80=9D
(inside of a word) or =E2=80=9Clow=E2=80=9D (outside of a word), and so it =
counts rising
edges.</p>

<p><a href=3D"https://nullprogram.com/img/diagram/wordcount.dot"><img src=
=3D"https://nullprogram.com/img/diagram/wordcount.svg" alt=3D""></a></p>

<p>The counter is not <em>technically</em> part of the state machine =E2=80=
=94 though it
eventually overflows for practical reasons, it isn=E2=80=99t really =E2=80=
=9Cfinite=E2=80=9D =E2=80=94 but
is rather an external count of the times the state machine transitions
from low to high, which is the actual, useful output.</p>

<p><em>Reader challenge</em>: Find a slick, efficient way to encode all tho=
se code
points as a table rather than rely on whatever the compiler generates for
the <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">switch</code> (cha=
in of branches, jump table?).</p>

<h3 id=3D"coroutines-and-generators-as-state-machines">Coroutines and gener=
ators as state machines</h3>

<p>In languages that support them, state machines can be implemented using
coroutines, including generators. I do particularly like the idea of
<a href=3D"https://nullprogram.com/blog/2018/05/31/">compiler-synthesized c=
oroutines</a> as state machines, though this is a
rare treat. The state is implicit in the coroutine at each yield, so the
programmer doesn=E2=80=99t have to manage it explicitly. (Though often that
explicit control is powerful!)</p>

